A||||6||Philphine||bonding urethane and fiberglass||||1||
Z||000000||Philphine||05-08-2001||10:18 AM||||<BR>now that i've recieved my chin spoiler i'm about ready to possibly destroy it. i've seen a few bonded permanently to the facia. has anyone here done it? any tips you can pass along? im thinking i need to be care full as far as the difference in flexability, but what else might i need to consider? ||||reg||1
Z||000001||rogergarrison||05-08-2001||11:10 AM||||Bonding any two dissimilar materials never works, no matter what they say. in a word 'dont'||||reg||1
Z||000002||AusFiero||05-08-2001||12:18 PM||||We have a product here called Araldite KP36.<BR>It will bond fibreglass to anything.<BR>Good example was an old boss of mine stuck his flares on his race car with it then moulded them in. (fibreglass to metal) During an accident a section of metal got torn away. Fiberglass section still tightly bonded to it. Shattered everywhere but where it joined. It is also sandable like car body filler so you don't have to use any other filler before painting.<P>Don't know if you have that product there but it highly recommended.<P>Also use lots of bolts no matter which way you bond it...it is an area prone to hits.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero/ausfiero.gif"> <BR>When I was young I spent 80% of my money on Women, Gambling and Alcohol. I guess I wasted the rest.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Taijiguy||05-08-2001||01:12 PM||||Try the flexible epoxy bumper repair kit. You're only going to have a small seam to fill. If not that, then you can at least fill a portion of the seam with flexible automotive seam filler. I comes in a tube like caulk. You need to have a regualr caulk gun, but it works good for filling those kinds of seams, but it won't really smooth or feather, but it will fill.<BR>What's the quality of that spoiler anyway?<BR>~Taiji||||reg||1
Z||000004||Spektyr||05-08-2001||01:26 PM||||I wouldn't actually recommend this, but adhesive caulk will bond fiberglass to painted metal pretty damn well.<P>I had a truck with a camper top that got into a wreck.  The body shop removed the topper to repair the side, and then used adhesive caulk to seal the topper to the rails of the bed before clamping the topper down.<P>When I went to sell the topper and replace it with a tonnaeu (sp?) cover I found I couldn't get the topper off the truck.  Two guys standing in the bed, planting their shoulders on the bottom side of the topper and then pushing upward with their legs couldn't get it free.<P>Like I said though, I wouldn't recommend it, but it sure as hell stuck that topper to the truck...||||reg||1
Z||000005||rogergarrison||05-08-2001||03:26 PM||||Ive never seen that K36 stuff aus. Maybe its not avail in US. Ive just seen so many jobs with molded flares, hood scoops, etc. They stay for a while, but always end up cracking somewhere in the joint. So I never even try. Only thing that ever worked was welding a metal scoop or flare to a metal body, but even then the bondo used to fill it ends up cracking sooner or later.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Philphine||05-08-2001||08:14 PM||||taji, it's not too bad. there are a couple of flaws i've noticed but all in all it's about as good as anything i've gotten from irm or the mustang wings i've gotten.<P>can't help myself. i've already cut my facia to make one big opening. i like how it looks. no turning back now. i think i can do it without actually filling in the seam but it would look more compleat if i can. <P>put my question on the vette forum too to see if they'll give me an answer. i figure they've been working with these materials longer.<P>aus, i'll look around for the stuff you mentioned, too.<p>[This message has been edited by Philphine (edited 05-08-2001).]||||reg||1
